<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dalit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Harijan, Panchama, Untouchables</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dalit, from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> word meaning broken or scattered, is the name of the people once called untouchables and outcastes, the lowest stratum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, excluded from the fourfold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> caste hierarchy and seen as a fifth, the Panchama. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> used the word in the late 1880s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, himself a Dalit, popularised it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s 1950 Constitution abolished untouchability and created reservations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a> in legislatures, government jobs and education, and in 1997 K. R. Narayanan became the first Dalit president. Caste discrimination nonetheless remains widespread, above all in rural India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dalit.jpg" alt="Dalit" width="330" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lady Ottoline Morrell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_school_of_untouchables_near_Bangalore_by_Lady_Ottoline_Morrell_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the 19th century; in 1956 B. R. Ambedkar, the jurist and campaigner for the rights of the <b>Dalits</b>, became a Buddhist at Nagpur with hundreds of thousands of followers; and in the 20th century Buddhist teachers and Asian migrants established communities in Europe, North America and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Critics of its view of caste include the economist Jean Drèze, who calls the Hindutva project “a lifeboat for the upper castes”, and the <b>Dalit</b> activist Kancha Ilaiah, for whom “Hindutva Is Nothing But Brahminism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The varna scheme of the ancient texts placed the Brahmins, the priests, at the head of four orders, above the Kshatriyas (rulers and warriors), the Vaishyas (traders) and the Shudras (labourers), with <b>Dalits</b> and Adivasis outside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier, in November 1896, the composer Julio Nakpil had written &quot;Marangal na <b>Dalit</b> ng Katagalugan&quot; as a national anthem for Andrés Bonifacio of the Katipunan, but Aguinaldo's governments never adopted it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing resistance to his campaign for the acceptance of <b>Dalits</b> in Hindu society, he cited it in his weekly Navajivan: a religion that had produced a devotee who saw all as equal could not hold some people untouchable.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vaishnava-jana-to">Vaishnava Jana To</a></li></ul></section>
